---
const threeTurnings = [
  {
    turning: "First Turning",
    focus: "Four Noble Truths & Conventional Reality",
    description:
      "Taught at Varanasi. Establishes the foundational ethics, causality (karma), impermanence, and selflessness of persons to abandon coarse grasping.",
  },
  {
    turning: "Second Turning",
    focus: "Prajnaparamita & Emptiness of Inherent Nature (Rangtong)",
    description:
      "Taught at Vulture Peak. Demonstrates that all compounded phenomena, concepts, and conventional entities lack independent, inherent existence.",
  },
  {
    turning: "Third Turning",
    focus: "Tathagatagarbha & Definitive Other-Emptiness (Shentong)",
    description:
      "Taught at Vaishali and other sites. Elucidates the luminous, uncompounded nature of primordial wisdom (jnana), free from adventitious defilements yet complete with inseparable enlightened qualities.",
  },
];

const lineagePillars = [
  {
    title: "Indian Masters & Transmissions",
    content:
      "Rooted in the treatises of Maitreya (Uttaratantra, Mahayanasutralankara) and Asanga, transmitted through Indian Mahasiddhas including Shavaripa, Maitripa, and Ghawadra before crossing the Himalayas.",
  },
  {
    title: "Early Tibetan Translators",
    content:
      "Translated and preserved in Tibet by Yumo Mikyo Dorje and Dro Lotsawa Shesrab Drak, who unified the Kalachakra Mulatantra with the definitive Shentong view of ultimate reality.",
  },
  {
    title: "Dolpopa Sherab Gyaltsen (1292–1361)",
    content:
      "The omniscient master who systematically articulated the Shentong Madhyamaka in his masterpiece 'Mountain Doctrine' (Ri chos nges don rgya mtsho) and constructed the Great Kumbum of Jomonang.",
  },
  {
    title: "Jetsun Taranatha (1575–1634)",
    content:
      "Prolific historian, tantric adept, and scholar who revitalized the Jonang lineage, founded Takten Damcho Ling Monastery, and composed definitive meditation manuals for the Kalachakra Six Yogas.",
  },
];
---

<div class="space-y-12">
  <!-- Core Philosophical Vision Callout -->
  <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10 space-y-6">
    <div class="badge badge-crimson">
      <span class="badge-dot"></span>
      Great Madhyamaka
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl font-display text-ink font-normal leading-tight">
      The Shentong (Other-Empty) View of Ultimate Reality
    </h2>

    <p class="lead text-base sm:text-lg text-muted">
      The Jonang school represents the definitive exponent of the <em>Shentong</em> (Other-Emptiness) Great Middle Way, holding that while conventional phenomena are empty of inherent nature (<em>rangtong</em>), the ultimate reality—Buddha-nature (<em>Tathagatagarbha</em>)—is empty only of adventitious, transient obscurations (<em>shentong</em>).
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink">
      Rather than viewing emptiness as sheer non-existence or mere conceptual negation, Jonang masters articulate ultimate truth as non-dual, primordial luminous awareness (<em>prabhasvara-jnana</em>), perpetually endowed with unconditioned wisdom, compassion, and enlightened attributes inseparable from mind's true nature.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- The Three Turnings Framework -->
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <div class="badge badge-saffron">
      <span class="badge-dot"></span>
      Scriptural Hermeneutics
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-display text-ink font-normal">
      The Three Turnings of the Dharma Wheel
    </h3>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      {
        threeTurnings.map((t) => (
          <div class="surface-card p-6 flex flex-col justify-between space-y-4 border-t-4 border-t-accent">
            <div>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent block mb-1">
                {t.turning}
              </span>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-2">{t.focus}</h4>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{t.description}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Unbroken Lineage Progression -->
  <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 md:p-10 space-y-6">
    <div class="badge badge-lapis">
      <span class="badge-dot"></span>
      Lineage Line
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-display text-ink font-normal">
      Transmission Lineage & Key Masters
    </h3>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
      {
        lineagePillars.map((pillar) => (
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border space-y-2">
            <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-bold text-ink">{pillar.title}</h4>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">{pillar.content}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Kalachakra Tantra Integration -->
  <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-2xl font-display text-ink">Union of Sutra and Tantra</h3>
    <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-muted leading-relaxed">
      In the Jonang tradition, high philosophical discourse is never separated from contemplative practice. The Shentong philosophical view directly informs and finds its experiential realization in the <strong>Six-fold Vajrayoga</strong> of the Kalachakra completion stage. Through systematic physical posture, breath retention, and subtle body yogas, the practitioner directly discovers the immutable bliss and emptiness articulated in the root sutras and tantras.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
